WEBVTT

00:00:09.221 --> 00:00:14.721
Hello, Lin Chan, Sales Manager for National
Sugar - and my associate John Martin.

00:00:14.721 --> 00:00:19.092
Very pleased to meet you. I'm Victor Tang,
and this is my legal adviser Sue Panay.

00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:20.801
I hope you had a pleasant flight over.

00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:21.851
Yes, we did thanks.

00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:23.211
Are you staying for a few days?

00:00:23.211 --> 00:00:25.601
Unfortunately we need to get back to Manila
tomorrow.

00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:36.431
Well, we'd better get down to business. Mr
Tang, to start off with, I just want to say

00:00:36.431 --> 00:00:41.552
we believe we can offer you a very good deal
and come up with a win-win result.

00:00:41.552 --> 00:00:46.482
Well, from our point of view, we see it as
an exploratory talk - testing the water you

00:00:46.482 --> 00:00:47.482
might say.

00:00:47.482 --> 00:00:51.942
We don't intend to reach any agreements at
this meeting - in any case we would need to

00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:53.651
run it past our board first.

00:00:53.651 --> 00:00:56.812
You haven't heard our terms yet - you may
find them hard to resist!

00:00:56.812 --> 00:00:58.641
(Victor and Sue exchange a raised eyebrow)

00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:03.921
Of course we understand you need time to consider
any offer. My first priority is to keep the

00:01:03.921 --> 00:01:06.692
negotiations open.

00:01:06.692 --> 00:01:08.522
What's your proposal Ms Chan?

00:01:08.522 --> 00:01:13.491
We're prepared to offer a very attractive
price for a minimum sale, in exchange for

00:01:13.491 --> 00:01:25.391
a two-year contract. John will clarify the
terms.

00:01:25.391 --> 00:01:31.552
A negotiation is a discussion that should
result in an agreement or business contract.

00:01:31.552 --> 00:01:37.402
The discussion is usually between two parties
- or organisations - trying to reach an agreement

00:01:37.402 --> 00:01:40.321
satisfactory to both.

00:01:40.321 --> 00:01:45.501
In a negotiation, we need to reach a position
that it is not too difficult for either side

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:54.152
to accept, so the language we use is important
- and it's also important to listen carefully.

00:01:54.152 --> 00:02:00.601
When starting a negotiation, begin with a
greeting, and what we call 'small-talk' - something

00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:04.082
to 'break the ice', or make the meeting a
friendly one.

00:02:04.082 --> 00:02:09.692
Hello, Lin Chan, Sales Manager for National
Sugar, and my associate John Martin.

00:02:09.692 --> 00:02:14.051
Very pleased to meet you. I'm Victor Tang,
and this is my legal adviser Sue Panay.

00:02:14.051 --> 00:02:15.762
I hope you had a pleasant flight over.

00:02:15.762 --> 00:02:16.932
Yes, we did thanks.

00:02:16.932 --> 00:02:18.182
Are you staying for a few days?

00:02:18.182 --> 00:02:21.342
Unfortunately we need to get back to Manila
tomorrow.

00:02:21.342 --> 00:02:27.872
Lin starts by introducing herself and her
associate. It's important that everyone at

00:02:27.872 --> 00:02:35.782
the meeting knows their roles, so Victor also
introduces Sue by telling them her job.

00:02:35.782 --> 00:02:41.702
Before they start the negotiations, John asks
about their journey. He says, "I hope you

00:02:41.702 --> 00:02:47.672
had a pleasant flight", and asks how long
they are staying. This way, the negotiation

00:02:47.672 --> 00:02:51.672
starts in a relaxed and friendly atmosphere.

00:02:51.672 --> 00:02:56.212
Here are a few phrases you could use to put
the other party at ease.

00:02:56.212 --> 00:03:01.052
I hope you had a pleasant flight.

00:03:01.052 --> 00:03:02.962
Are you enjoying Sydney?

00:03:02.962 --> 00:03:10.932
How is your hotel?
In some cultures, it is usual not to talk

00:03:10.932 --> 00:03:15.963
about business straight away, but in others,
it is expected that you will get down to business

00:03:15.963 --> 00:03:22.932
quickly. You just have to judge the situation,
and feel your own way.

00:03:22.932 --> 00:03:27.162
How does Lin signal that it is time to start
the negotiation?

00:03:27.162 --> 00:03:32.662
Well, we'd better get down to business.
Lin says, "We'd better get down to business".

00:03:32.662 --> 00:03:39.642
Notice that she makes it a suggestion. She
is suggesting they should talk business.

00:03:39.642 --> 00:03:43.732
Practise some phrases for suggesting it's
time to talk business.

00:03:43.732 --> 00:03:49.332
Well, we'd better get down to business.

00:03:49.332 --> 00:03:53.982
Shall we get down to business?

00:03:53.982 --> 00:04:02.082
Well, how about we get down to business?
The next thing Lin does is make an opening

00:04:02.082 --> 00:04:07.452
statement. An opening statement should tell
the other person what you are expecting to

00:04:07.452 --> 00:04:13.352
get out of the meeting. The opening statement
would normally be made by the person who requested

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:17.101
the meeting.
Mr Tang, to start off with, I just want to

00:04:17.101 --> 00:04:22.071
say we believe we can offer you a very good
deal and come up with a win-win result.

00:04:22.072 --> 00:04:29.352
Lin says, "to start off with" and then she
states what she wants to achieve. She says

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:34.602
she is going to offer "a very good deal",
and that she wants to achieve a "win-win"

00:04:34.602 --> 00:04:40.202
result. She is signalling to the other party
that she wants both of them to be happy with

00:04:40.202 --> 00:04:47.692
the outcome. Practise, with Lin, some phrases
to introduce an opening statement.

00:04:47.692 --> 00:04:53.091
Let me start off by saying

00:04:53.091 --> 00:04:58.521
I'd like to begin by saying

00:04:58.521 --> 00:05:06.972
Let me kick things off by saying
To 'kick things off' is to start a discussion.

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:12.472
How do Victor and Sue respond?
Well, from our point of view, we see it as

00:05:12.472 --> 00:05:15.391
an exploratory talk - testing the water you
might say.

00:05:15.391 --> 00:05:19.942
We don't intend to reach any agreements at
this meeting - in any case we would need to

00:05:19.942 --> 00:05:25.452
run it past our board first.
Victor says, "from our point of view", and

00:05:25.452 --> 00:05:33.822
Lin says, "we don't intend". They use the
words 'our' and 'we', instead of 'my' and

00:05:33.822 --> 00:05:41.181
'I' because they are talking as representatives
of the company, not as individuals. If Victor

00:05:41.181 --> 00:05:49.282
was on his own, he might use 'I' and 'my'
- especially as the C.E.O. of the company.

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:54.722
Here are some other phrases Victor could use
to state their point of view:

00:05:54.722 --> 00:06:01.961
From our perspective
Our position is that

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:06.421
As far as we're concerned

00:06:06.421 --> 00:06:12.782
Victor says they see it as an 'exploratory
talk'. He means they are 'exploring options',

00:06:12.782 --> 00:06:18.931
or finding out what Lin has to offer. He is
suggesting by this that they aren't going

00:06:18.931 --> 00:06:25.532
to make a decision at this meeting - and he
is letting Lin know this. He describes this

00:06:25.532 --> 00:06:30.761
in another way by saying they are 'testing
the water'.

00:06:30.761 --> 00:06:37.841
Notice too that Sue reinforces this. She says,
"We don't intend to reach any agreements at

00:06:37.841 --> 00:06:45.822
this meeting." She is stating clearly the
outcome that they are expecting from the meeting.

00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:52.521
She says they would have to "run past the
board" any proposals made. A proposal is a

00:06:52.521 --> 00:06:59.162
formal offer or suggestion made by one business
to another, and to 'run something past the

00:06:59.162 --> 00:07:06.791
board' means to get the board's approval or
feedback. In a negotiation, each party needs

00:07:06.791 --> 00:07:12.472
to respond to what the other says for the
negotiation to proceed.

00:07:12.472 --> 00:07:18.621
How does Lin respond to Victor and Sue's statement?
Of course we understand you need time to consider

00:07:18.621 --> 00:07:23.151
any offer. My first priority is to keep the
negotiations open.

00:07:23.151 --> 00:07:30.151
She says she understands they are not going
to agree at this meeting. She says, "My first

00:07:30.151 --> 00:07:38.761
priority is to keep the negotiations open."
A priority is an important goal. A first priority

00:07:38.761 --> 00:07:42.072
is your most important goal.

00:07:42.072 --> 00:07:47.502
Notice the reaction when John speaks.
We don't intend to reach any agreements at

00:07:47.502 --> 00:07:51.961
this meeting - in any case we would need to
run it past our board first.

00:07:51.961 --> 00:07:57.101
You haven't heard our terms yet - you may
find them hard to resist!

00:07:57.101 --> 00:08:03.331
John hasn't listened to what Victor and Sue
have said, and they don't like it. But Lin

00:08:03.331 --> 00:08:09.671
makes a conciliatory statement. That is, she
makes a concession. She backs down from her

00:08:09.671 --> 00:08:16.701
first position. When negotiating, you usually
need to make some concessions to reach an

00:08:16.701 --> 00:08:24.162
agreement. If nobody makes a concession, the
negotiation can't proceed. Like this:

00:08:24.162 --> 00:08:29.121
I just want to say we believe we can offer
you a very good deal and come up with a win-win

00:08:29.121 --> 00:08:30.252
result.

00:08:30.252 --> 00:08:35.001
Well, from our point of view, we see it as
an exploratory talk - testing the water you

00:08:35.001 --> 00:08:36.121
might say.

00:08:36.121 --> 00:08:39.311
I'm sure we'll be able to resolve everything
today.

00:08:39.312 --> 00:08:42.011
We need to run anything past our board first.

00:08:42.011 --> 00:08:47.102
Why bother the board? We can settle this deal
right now!

00:08:47.102 --> 00:08:51.762
I'm afraid that won't be possible.

00:08:51.762 --> 00:08:57.012
If we don't listen carefully to what the other
party is signalling, negotiations can break

00:08:57.012 --> 00:09:04.032
down very quickly. Now that each side has
made their position clear, they can talk about

00:09:04.032 --> 00:09:08.642
the details of the proposal.
What's your proposal Ms Chan?

00:09:08.642 --> 00:09:13.612
We're prepared to offer a very attractive
price for a minimum sale, in exchange for

00:09:13.612 --> 00:09:18.392
a two-year contract. John will clarify the
terms.

00:09:18.392 --> 00:09:24.652
Let's review the main points from today. In
negotiations, begin with introductions and

00:09:24.652 --> 00:09:32.452
then some informal talk. Then each side makes
an opening statement -this should state clearly

00:09:32.452 --> 00:09:35.712
what they want to achieve.

00:09:35.712 --> 00:09:42.632
Then, whichever party called the meeting begins
the negotiation by giving an opening proposal.

00:09:42.632 --> 00:09:48.032
And remember, it's important to listen to
signals and the opening statements carefully,

00:09:48.032 --> 00:09:53.802
otherwise the negotiation can quickly go in
the wrong direction.

00:09:53.802 --> 00:09:56.852
That's all for The Business of English for
today. See you next time.